    "body": "> [!WARNING]\n> **Needs a second opinion on one thing:** commit 1 switches `MenhirCorpus` from\n> `Parser.to_zipper` to `Parser.to_segment`, and I am **not confident that is safe\n> in general** \u2014 only that it holds across today's corpus. See\n> \"[The part I'm unsure about](#the-part-im-unsure-about)\". The slide split\n> (commit 2) is independent and can land without it.\n\n## What\n\n**Splits the B2T2 slides per operation, bringing them in line with the current\nversion of the benchmark.** The committed slides grouped several categories into\none file each \u2014 `Aggregate`, `Subtable`, `Properties`, `Ordering`,\n`MissingValues`, `DataCleaning`, `AccessSubcomponents`, and the three Errors\n\"parts\" \u2014 while the artifact's lab sources keep one file per operation. This\nregenerates `hazel-programs/docs/b2t2` from those sources: **39 files \u2192 62**, one\nslide per operation, and picks up the upstream slide edits along with the split.\n\nThe menu therefore goes from 39 to 62 entries. That's the user-visible part of\nthis PR and the bit worth an opinion.\n\nNames and menu order come from the manifest in `sync-slides.sh` (B2T2 operation\norder, not alphabetical). The 28 slides that were already one-per-operation keep\ntheir existing filenames, so the churn is limited to the 10 grouped files and the\n33 replacing them.\n\n## Why it also makes the suite much faster\n\nThe grouped files were the largest programs in the corpus \u2014 `table-api-aggregate.hz`\nalone was 10.5 KB \u2014 and the editor parser is superlinear: `to_segment` only splits\nat top-level boundaries, so within a segment cost still grows faster than linearly.\nRe-splitting the same content (largest file now 6.7 KB, total bytes essentially\nunchanged) cut `MarkerParse.of_text` over the corpus by **39%** and\n`Parser.to_segment` by **32%**.\n\nSo this is faster while testing *more*: the suite gains 69 cases, because 62\nslides generate more per-slide checks than 39 did.\n\n| group | before | after |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| `MenhirCorpus` | 90.4 s | 21.2 s |\n| `DocSlides.ReparseBackuptext` | 52.7 s | 11.7 s |\n| `TextRoundtrip.DocSlides` | 103.4 s | 78.0 s |\n| **full suite (local)** | **345.5 s** | **211.0 s** (\u221238.9%) |\n| test cases | 3496 | 3565 |\n\nMeasured locally with a `junit_alcotest` patched to record real per-case times \u2014\nupstream hardcodes `~time:0.`, which is why none of this was visible from the\nexisting reports.\n\n## Commit 1: the test-side parse changes\n\nTwo changes, independent of the slide split:\n\n**`MenhirCorpus` parses via `to_segment` instead of `to_zipper`** (90.4 s \u2192 37.2 s\non the old corpus). See the warning below.\n\n**`MenhirCorpus` and `DocSlides.ReparseBackuptext` share the parse.** They turn out\nto check the same programs \u2014 the corpus files *are* the slide sources \u2014 and both\nneed the typing parser's segment, which each was paying for separately.\n`test/ParsedCorpus.re` memoizes it, taking `ReparseBackuptext` from 51.7 s to\n11.5 s. Making the cache hit required `MenhirCorpus` to normalize the way the load\npath does (dedent every line, strip one trailing newline) rather than\n`String.trim`, since otherwise the two callers key different strings for the same\nprogram. That drops the indented-form differential \u2014 narrow, and a shape\nproduction never produces, since every slide is dedented on load.\n\nWorth recording that the original hypothesis here was wrong: the two *slide*\ngroups looked redundant, but `DocSlides` spends its time in `MarkerParse.of_text`\nand `ReparseBackuptext` in `Parser.to_segment`, and the one call they share costs\nabout a tenth of a second. The real duplication was with `MenhirCorpus`.\n\n## The part I'm unsure about\n\n`Parser.to_zipper` is the **definitional** editor parse \u2014 it replays what the\neditor does per keystroke. `Parser.to_segment` is an *optimization* over it: it\nsplits the input at points it believes are safe, strips the convex grout artifact\n`Zipper.init()` leaves behind, then does a final top-level regrout to reconcile\nshapes across boundaries. Any of those three steps could plausibly differ from\nto_zipper for a program shape not in today's corpus.\n\n**What I did verify**, because it's the failure mode that would hide itself:\n`check_file` silently passes when the editor parser returns `None`, so a file that\n`to_segment` rejected would have dropped out of coverage with the suite still\ngreen. That does not happen \u2014 across all corpus files both entry points accept\nevery file and produce structurally equal terms.\n\n**What I did not verify** is that the two agree in general. The consequence is\nthat `MenhirCorpus` now pins menhir against the optimized parser rather than the\nground-truth one, so if `to_segment` ever drifts from `to_zipper`, this test would\ncompare against the wrong baseline and not notice.\n\nThe invariant \"`to_segment` \u2261 `to_zipper` corpus-wide\" is worth asserting, but\ndoing so costs about what using `to_zipper` here would, which is the entire saving\n\u2014 so it belongs in a periodic run rather than per-PR. That's noted in the file's\nheader. **If reviewers would rather not take this on faith, commit 1's swap can be\ndropped and the shared-parse half kept; the slide split carries most of the win\nregardless.**\n\n## `known_gaps` fallout, which is an improvement\n\nThe single `table-api-properties.hz` gap entry was tolerating three operations at\nonce. Split, the `[()]` tokenizer quirk turns out to affect `header` and `ncols`\nbut **not** `nrows` \u2014 so the ledger now names the two files that actually carry it,\nand `nrows` is genuinely checked for the first time. No new gaps.\n\n## Verified\n\n- `make dev` builds the web bundle against the regenerated `Slides.re`\n- full suite green\n- `MenhirCorpus` green with the re-attributed ledger\n\n## Not addressed here\n\n`TextRoundtrip.DocSlides` is now the largest remaining group at 78 s, all of it in\n`MarkerParse.of_text` \u2192 `to_zipper`. Reimplementing that over `to_segment` is worth\nroughly 40 s more, but it is production code (`PersistentZipper`'s fallback path),\nneeds a zipper rather than a segment, and also strips implicit holes and preserves\nrefractors \u2014 a real refactor rather than a substitution, so it is deliberately out\nof scope.\n\nSeparately: the `unused open EditingPrelude` warning in `Test_ReparseDocSlides.re`\nis untouched here. Its removal belongs to #2456, on #2422.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
